Before dawn on January 26, 1929, the aircraft carrier Saratoga turned into the wind roughly 140 miles from the Panama Canal and began launching planes.
About seventy aircraft rose from her deck in less than an hour, crossed the coast, and surprised the defenders in a United States Navy exercise known as Fleet Problem IX.
The umpires judged the canal locks destroyed, a result that seemed to announce a new way of projecting naval power.
Later in the same exercise, battleships found the lightly escorted Saratoga at close range, and a submarine also reached a firing position, so the umpires ruled the carrier sunk more than once.
The same ship had demonstrated both the extraordinary reach of concentrated naval aviation and the danger of concentrating so much capability in one conspicuous hull.
That tension has followed the aircraft carrier ever since.
A carrier can move an airfield across the sea, but it also gathers aircraft, fuel, ammunition, workshops, communications, and thousands of trained people into a single location.
Calling it either a floating fortress or an oversized target misses the harder fact that its value and vulnerability belong to a system.
Saratoga had made her surprise possible by detaching from the slower battle fleet with only the cruiser Omaha, taking an indirect route, and relying on speed and secrecy.
Those choices also stripped away much of the protection and scouting that a carrier normally needed to survive.

Even the word carrier encourages a misleading picture, because the hull merely carries the components from which a military capability must be assembled.
Aircraft require mechanics, spare parts, fuel of the right kind, weapons, elevators, hangars, launch equipment, recovery equipment, weather information, and people able to coordinate a crowded deck without fatal confusion.
The ship requires escorts against threats from the air, surface, and underwater, as well as tankers, supply ships, repair yards, and secure places where crews and air groups can train.
Aircraft and ship must also fit each other in weight, dimensions, landing speed, elevator capacity, deck strength, and the forces that catapults and arresting gear can impose.
A mismatch at any of these interfaces can reduce an impressive aircraft or vessel to an expensive piece of unavailable potential.
The platform is therefore not the capability, just as a theater is not a play.
Unlike a play, however, carrier operations cannot be reconstructed quickly by handing new scripts to the same cast.
Pilots must acquire a specialized skill, deck crews must learn tightly sequenced routines, commanders must develop doctrine, and shipyards must retain the equipment and knowledge to repair machinery found nowhere else.
Repeated operation improves this network, while each improvement makes continued investment in compatible ships and aircraft more attractive.
That feedback is the beginning of path dependence: earlier choices alter the cost, probability, and visibility of later ones.

The first American carrier made this process unusually visible because it began as something else.
The Navy converted the collier Jupiter into Langley, commissioned her as a carrier in 1922, and used the ungainly ship as a laboratory for operations that no manual had yet stabilized.
On Langley, aviators and deck crews experimented with parking, launch tempo, arresting wires, catapults, and the new role of a landing signal officer who guided aircraft during their final approach.
The resulting knowledge did not reside in the hull alone.
It traveled through trained people, reports, exercises, procurement requirements, and routines that later ships could inherit.
Langley consequently created options even as it narrowed them, because every reliable procedure made some designs easier to operate and incompatible designs harder to introduce.
This was a useful binding rather than a design failure.
Without common routines, a flight deck would have remained a dangerous collection of individual improvisations, and the carrier could not have developed into a repeatable fleet capability.
The bind became stronger when lessons learned aboard one ship shaped the wider dimensions, equipment, and organization of its successors.
An experimental platform had begun to generate an institutional platform.

The next step was not selected from a blank page.
The Washington Naval Treaty of 1922 limited capital-ship and carrier tonnage but allowed the United States and Japan to convert two unfinished battle-cruiser or battleship hulls into carriers.
The United States chose the partially built Lexington and Saratoga, gaining large, fast ships capable of carrying substantial air groups while also inheriting structures and machinery conceived for another purpose.
Other paths remained possible, including smaller purpose-built carriers, continued dependence on battleships, or different mixtures of shore-based and shipborne aviation.
Budget limits, treaty rules, available hulls, and incomplete knowledge changed which of those paths could be tried at useful scale.
When Ranger, the first American carrier designed from the keel up, entered service in the 1930s, it did not erase the operational knowledge accumulated on the conversions.
Design and doctrine now developed together: ships enabled certain patterns of flight, exercises rewarded some patterns, and those patterns informed requirements for later ships.
This reciprocity matters because it prevents a simple story in which a superior machine arrives and forces an organization to modernize.
Technology helped define what sailors could do, while learned practice helped define what engineers were asked to build.
Neither side of that relationship was fixed.

Fleet Problem IX accelerated the feedback without settling it.
The dawn strike showed that a fast carrier could act as the center of an offensive formation rather than merely spotting for battleships.
The repeated simulated losses showed that range without protection could become exposure, prompting stronger attention to escorts, scouting, fighter defense, and the organization of a carrier task force.
The exercise thus enlarged the carrier system in response to the very vulnerability that the carrier's success had revealed.
It also exposed a less dramatic constraint: fuel.
To launch and recover aircraft, a carrier had to steam at speed into the wind and then maneuver to rejoin ships following another course, a rhythm that consumed fuel far faster than cruising calculations suggested.
During later fleet exercises, Lexington and Saratoga demonstrated that operations far from bases would require more capacity and dependable replenishment at sea.
A ship intended to extend naval reach therefore pulled tankers, procedures, hoses, trained crews, and scheduling into its supporting network.
The farther the airfield moved from land, the more infrastructure had to move with it or wait along its route.
Mobility was not independence from infrastructure but a particular way of distributing infrastructure across an ocean.

This distinction clarifies what a system trap can and cannot mean.
Money already spent on a hull is a sunk cost, irrecoverable whether the navy keeps the ship or retires it, so past expenditure alone cannot establish the value of another deployment or modernization.
Real switching costs face forward: replacing the carrier may also require new aircraft, bases, escorts, maintenance facilities, training pipelines, doctrine, and enough transitional capacity to avoid losing a capability before its substitute works.
Those costs can justify continuity even when decision makers feel no sentimental attachment to the existing platform.
They can also conceal escalation of commitment if officials count every supporting investment as a reason never to reconsider the whole arrangement.
Path dependence identifies the changed structure of choice; it does not decide whether remaining on the path is wise.
That judgment must compare future missions, risks, alternatives, and transition costs rather than treating longevity as either proof of fitness or evidence of folly.
Carriers made this judgment especially difficult because their concentration of capability produced both operational flexibility and a recurring fear that one successful attack could remove an enormous share of a fleet's striking power.
The fear was not an invention of a later missile age, for the exercises that helped create carrier doctrine had already displayed the problem before carriers fought their first great battles.
Yet every measure adopted to protect the carrier would thicken the surrounding system and change the terms of the next vulnerability test.
